MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: aa9506f8b8c580c448622cdfc193780a, type: 3}
  m_Name: Case2_Lighthouse
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CaseDefinition
  CaseId: lighthouse
  Number: 2
  Title: The Lighthouse at Gull Point
  Location: "The Lamp Room  \xB7  A Storm at Midnight"
  SceneName: Lighthouse
  MenuBlurb: The light went dark in a storm, a ship full of silver struck the rocks,
    and the keeper lay dead at the foot of the stairs.
  Intro: 'At 11:52 last night the Gull Point light went dark. Eighteen minutes later
    the schooner Merrow struck the rocks called the Teeth, her cargo of silver spilling
    into the surf.


    Keeper Silas Marsh was found at the foot of the lamp-room
    stairs at half past midnight. The harbour board says he fell, rushing to relight
    the lamp. The storm is still raging. Find out what really happened up here.'
  Clues:
  - Id: LH_FUEL_VALVE
    Riddle: My wheel is small but I hold the light's breath. Turn me shut and the
      whole sea goes dark. Look low on the lamp's iron leg.
    Tip: 'The lamp didn''t run out of oil. Look at the fuel valve on the lamp''s
      pedestal: the little brass wheel facing the room.'
  - Id: LH_BUTTON
    Riddle: Anchor and crown, I hang where a man went down. Look on the iron ring
      that guards the stairs.
    Tip: Check the railing around the stair hatch. Something from somebody's coat
      got caught there tonight.
  - Id: LH_SPYGLASS
    Riddle: My eye is long and my tube is brass. I stare at the rocks from the ledge
      by the glass.
    Tip: There's a spyglass resting on the window ledge, pointed straight at the
      rocks. See where it's aimed.
  - Id: LH_MATCHES
    Riddle: Three little sticks with their heads burned black, dropped by the lamp
      by someone who came back.
    Tip: Look on the floor at the base of the great lamp, near the fuel tank. Someone
      dropped some matches.
  - Id: LH_WATCH
    Riddle: My face is cracked and my hands stood still when a body fell down the
      stairs. Look near the hole in the floor.
    Tip: There's a pocket watch on the floor near the stair hatch. Read the time
      it stopped.
  - Id: LH_LOGBOOK
    Riddle: I keep the hours of the lamp in blue cloth. The keeper's last words sit
      on his writing shelf.
    Tip: Silas's logbook is on his writing shelf by the window. Read the last entry.
  - Id: LH_MANIFEST
    Riddle: 'Sealed in red, I list what the sea now keeps: forty silver bars beneath
      the deeps. I lie on the keeper''s shelf.'
    Tip: On the writing shelf there's a folded paper with a red seal. It explains
      why someone wanted that ship on the rocks.
  - Id: LH_PIPE
    Riddle: White and thin with a cold little bowl, I wait on the shelf for my owner's
      return. Was he even here?
    Tip: The clay pipe on the writing shelf belongs to the assistant keeper. Check
      whether it was smoked tonight.
  CluesRequiredToAccuse: 5
  MaxAccusations: 3
  Questions:
  - Prompt: Who killed Silas Marsh?
    Options:
    - Jonas Reed, the harbourmaster
    - Edwin Pike, the assistant
    - Nell Marsh, his daughter
    - 'No one: he fell'
    CorrectIndex: 0
  - Prompt: Why did the light go out at 11:52?
    Options:
    - The fuel valve was shut on purpose
    - The wick burned out
    - The storm broke the glass
    - Silas forgot to fill the tank
    CorrectIndex: 0
  - Prompt: Why was it done?
    Options:
    - To wreck the Merrow for her silver
    - To inherit the keeper's cottage
    - To become head keeper
    - Revenge for an old debt
    CorrectIndex: 0
  - Prompt: When did Silas die?
    Options:
    - 11:49, before the light went out
    - 12:10, when the ship struck
    - 12:30, when he was found
    - 11:30, after trimming the wick
    CorrectIndex: 0
  SolutionText: 'Harbourmaster <b>Jonas Reed</b> wanted the Merrow on the rocks.
    A wreck in his waters meant a third of her silver for the harbour board, and
    a fat cut for himself.


    At 11:44 he climbed the tower again and told Silas
    to let the light fail. Silas refused. They struggled at the stair rail, Reed''s
    button tore away, and Silas went down the stairs. His watch stopped at <b>11:49</b>.


    At
    11:52 Reed screwed the <b>fuel valve</b> shut and watched through the spyglass
    until the Merrow struck the Teeth. Then he relit the lamp with matches from the
    Anchor & Lamp, so everyone would say the light never failed and the keeper simply
    fell.


    Edwin was ringing the fog bell all night, and Nell was on the mainland.'
  FailLine: Your accusation sinks like the Merrow. Jonas Reed signs the salvage papers
    at dawn, and the harbour board shares out the silver.
  TopRankName: Keeper of the Light
  WrenCallOut: Psst... detective. Over here, out of the wind.
  WrenIntro: Don't look so surprised, detective. Wherever there's money and a body,
    you'll find me. Tonight it's forty bars of silver at the bottom of the bay.
  WrenWhoAmI: Wren. I watch things for people who pay. Tonight I was paid to see
    the Merrow come in safe. She didn't, so somebody owes my client a great deal
    of silver. Help me find out who.
  WrenSuspectsLine: Three people had business with Silas tonight. Pick one.
  Suspects:
  - Name: Jonas Reed
    Role: the harbourmaster
    Intel: Runs the harbour, and the harbour board's salvage rights. Anything that
      washes off a wreck in his waters, the board takes a third, and Reed takes his
      cut. He's been up this tower twice this month, arguing with Silas about the
      light.
  - Name: Edwin Pike
    Role: the assistant keeper
    Intel: Silas's assistant. Ambitious, and Silas wouldn't recommend him for a light
      of his own. But tonight was his watch on the fog bell at the foot of the tower.
      You could hear him ringing it across the bay, every half minute, all night.
  - Name: Nell Marsh
    Role: the keeper's daughter
    Intel: Silas's daughter. They quarrelled because she wants to leave Gull Point
      for good, and the cottage comes to her now. But she took the evening ferry
      to the mainland. It left at nine, before the storm closed the harbour.
  DeductionTips:
  - 'You''ve got everything. Start with time: the watch, the logbook, and when the
    light went out. Who died first, the keeper or the light?'
  - Silas lit his lamp with flint, never matches. So who relit it after the wreck,
    and why would they want it burning when help arrived?
  - Follow the button and the silver. Only one name on that list profits from a wreck
    in his own harbour.
  WrenSolved: The Merrow's owners will want a word with Jonas Reed. So will I. Nice
    work, detective. Mind the stairs on your way down.
  FinchCallOut: Oi! Detective! Over here by the oil barrels, I'm soaked!
  FinchIntro: Finch! Remember me? I rowed out with the post boat and got stuck when
    the storm came in. Riddles are still free, detective. Wren charges, I don't.
  FinchWitness: I was shelterin' in the oil store at the bottom. Around a quarter
    to twelve a big man in a harbour coat went up the stairs, swearin'. Later I heard
    a crash out on the rocks, then somebody came down in a hurry, stinkin' of matches.
  FinchSolved: Reed?! I'll have it in every paper on the coast by breakfast. Mind
    you don't slip on the way down!
